  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Research Folio",
    "overview": "Warm paper surrounds an inset notebook surface. Forest-green controls, serif\nheadings and compact Geist text make the workspace feel personal. Real\nhandwriting remains the focus; source, correction and save state stay easy to\ncompare. This warmer treatment follows the user’s request for a cozier workspace\nand clearer selections.\n\nThis is the current **web** system. Its source of truth is the\n[web stylesheet](../../frontend/src/styles.css),\n[React shell](../../frontend/src/components/AppShell.tsx) and\n[review workspace](../../frontend/src/modules/mathNotes.ts). The frontmatter\nrecords implemented web tokens; `dark-` entries are the dark values of the same\nCSS roles. The [surface brief](../../.impeccable/surfaces/web-workspace.md) owns\npage composition. [Status](../STATUS.md) owns acceptance and remaining limits.\n\nNative iPhone/iPad retains the October 14 **Quiet Notebook** foundation: system\nfonts with Dynamic Type, warm gray canvas/sidebar, neutral prominent actions,\nSwiftUI navigation, sheets and PencilKit. Its\n[appearance implementation](../../ios/Sources/PersonalizedNotesApp/SettingsView.swift)\nand [dated evidence](../release-evidence/UI_REVAMP_2026-10-14.md) remain applicable.\nThe web palette, Geist font and folio geometry below do not migrate native UI.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Warm paper, soft green accents and a comfortable notebook surface.",
      "Forest-green actions, visibly selected controls and labeled feedback.",
      "Real page previews and a visible source-to-correction relationship.",
      "Explicit saving, recoverable drafts and deliberate training consent."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Original Fidelity Rule",
        "body": "Appearance changes interface chrome, never\nstored ink, scan pixels or export content. Original crops retain their own\ncolors and white paper. Notebook paper settings remain a separate concern.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Content Type Rule",
        "body": "Prose corrections use the interface face; LaTeX uses\nmonospace. Do not make ordinary notes look like source code.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Sheet Rule",
        "body": "Resting content is flat. Borders and surface changes\nestablish hierarchy; shadows belong to menus, page/view popovers and dialogs.\nCards gain a stronger border rather than lifting on hover. Floating surfaces\nare opaque, with restrained dark scrims behind modal dialogs. Exact shadow and\nmotion values live in the [design sidecar](../../.impeccable/design.json).",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Explicit Save Rule",
        "body": "Editing creates an in-memory draft, not a saved\nreview. Drafts survive workspace navigation while the page stays open; reload\nor closing the page can lose them. Preserve “Changes not saved”, copy recovery,\nconflict comparison and the deliberate discard/reconvert action. Export offers\n“Save and export” or “Export last saved version” when drafts exist.",
        "section": "components"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** use existing semantic colors and owned primitives in both appearances.",
      "**Do** keep original pixels, selected source and correction visibly connected.",
      "**Do** preserve explicit save, recovery, export scope and optional consent.",
      "**Do** retain visible focus, keyboard access, reduced motion and touch targets.",
      "**Do** inspect phone/desktop and light/dark captures after web UI changes."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** apply the web font or palette to native without a native change.",
      "**Don't** invert scans or imply that an unsaved draft is durable.",
      "**Don't** hide save failures or conflicts in transient feedback alone.",
      "**Don't** add gradients, glass panels or decorative cards around every region.",
      "**Don't** treat synthetic browser captures as model, hardware or native proof."
    ]
  }
}
